#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088647} :activity/id #uuid "dcdb474d-2893-54de-b9a6-fc351d677a1e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289 :topic/id #uuid "917bf85e-7253-5a9c-8395-ebac6e9e50d7" :topic/math-academy-id 880} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088942 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **complex roots**. Let's see how to find the solution in this case.\n\nWe will solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y =0.\n$$\n\n**Step 1:** Find the characteristic equation.\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ we find the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the ODE gives the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0\\,(\\text{since }{e}^{\\lambda x}\\ne 0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for the characteristic roots.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are a *complex conjugate pair*. Since the roots are distinct, the general solution is a linear combination of the corresponding exponentials:\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2\\textrm{i}x} + Be^{-2\\textrm{i}x}.\n$$\n\nOn the next slide, we will use *Euler's formula* to convert this into a more useful real-valued form." :tutorial/id #uuid "18a414e1-5fae-5260-8980-5971fbd844e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4052 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "597d4650-d7ed-5642-9b5e-cf25ec638f05" :step/math-academy-id 24233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087595 :question/id #uuid "07394b09-ab80-579c-9b10-dc7a2835d3c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6595"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049695}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424421}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"} :step/id #uuid "d74a7f00-bd78-50fa-b35a-fe22c92c7804" :step/math-academy-id 24234 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087596 :question/id #uuid "b22fa1be-4c82-5dbf-b5fe-5cbb42c0c646" :question/math-academy-id "e-6596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357484}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "59826183-4e40-52cf-85bf-49e587ced10a" :step/math-academy-id 24235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088943 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE may have complex solutions. In such cases, the general solution will initially involve complex exponentials.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 18y =0.\n$$\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}$, we get the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 18 =0.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by *completing the square*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two complex roots are $\\lambda_1 = 3 + 3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2 = 3 - 3\\textrm{i}.$\n\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{(3 + 3 \\textrm{i})x} + Be^{(3 - 3 \\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we will use *Euler's formula* to rewrite this solution in a form that does not involve complex numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "d2be766f-b054-5431-aaa5-32c2129b9d92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4053 :tutorial/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "31b88c4c-e4b0-5b75-8e76-d8013e805d0f" :step/math-academy-id 24236 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087597 :question/id #uuid "7aa1ff4a-2263-5c99-97dd-d6fcada8bfc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6597"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424420}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "22d3be62-0495-564f-8728-c69a96ff20e8" :step/math-academy-id 24237 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087598 :question/id #uuid "04253a00-f441-58d8-b97e-f7935ae5c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6598"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424422}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "87a6e8c8-6061-5698-9cbf-e13d800b6a02" :step/math-academy-id 24238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088957 :tutorial/content "In general, if the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **imaginary roots** \n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\pm b\\textrm{i},\n$$\n\nthen the *general solution* is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{\\textrm{i} b x} + B e^{-\\textrm{i} bx}.\n$$\n\nUsing *Euler's formula*, the above can be simplified to\n\n$$\ny(x) = P \\cos bx + Q \\sin bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $P = A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i} (A-B).$\n\nLet's take a look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0cc2f848-e126-5ee8-84ea-809e08a92dff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4162 :tutorial/title "A Shortcut When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"} :step/id #uuid "e724fe89-8251-5f08-9463-a1bf1a894289" :step/math-academy-id 25327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088958 :tutorial/content "In general, if the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **complex conjugate roots** \n\n$$\n\\lambda = a \\pm b\\textrm{i},\n$$\n\n then the *general solution* is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{(a+b\\textrm{i})x} + B e^{(a-b\\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nUsing *Euler's formula*, we can expand and group the terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{ax} \\left[ (A+B)\\cos bx + \\textrm{i}(A-B)\\sin bx \\right].\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{ax} \\left( P \\cos bx + Q \\sin bx \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P = A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i} (A-B).$\n\nLet's take a look at another example" :tutorial/id #uuid "922b0814-7e51-5155-9464-ccf52671087e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4163 :tutorial/title "A Shortcut When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "c889c748-9b68-50fe-a9f2-7282493522dd" :step/math-academy-id 25328 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088863 :tutorial/content "Recall that our general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{(3 + 3 \\textrm{i})x} + Be^{(3 - 3 \\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nOur goal is to simplify this to find the **real-valued general solution**. We can do this using **Euler's formula**, which states that \n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos{\\theta} + \\textrm{i}\\sin{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we use the law of exponents to separate the real and imaginary parts in the exponential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x}{e}^{3\\text{i}x}+B{e}^{3x}{e}^{-3\\text{i}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(A{e}^{3\\text{i}x}+B{e}^{-3\\text{i}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply Euler's formula with $\\theta = 3x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{3x}(A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}((A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants, we can define new arbitrary constants $P=A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i}(A-B).$ The general solution becomes:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y = e^{3x}(P\\cos{3 x} + Q\\sin{3 x}).}\n$$\n\nThis is the final form of the solution, written entirely with real-valued functions.\n\nSo, for example,\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{3x}(5\\cos{3 x} - \\sin{3 x}),\n$$\n\ncorresponding to $P=5$ and $Q=-1,$ is a solution of the given differential equation. On the other hand,\n\n$$\ny(x) =  e^{3x}(\\cos{5 x} - \\sin{3 x})\n$$\n\nis *not* a solution because it contains a function $e^{3x}\\cos{5x},$ which is not included in the above form representing the general solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "25452a20-5d48-511d-b978-9a5f1c2b8894" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14887 :tutorial/title "Convert the Complex Solution to a Real-Valued Solution"} :step/id #uuid "41c07f62-cd8a-521d-a6ef-1857ff038a27" :step/math-academy-id 607946 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088864 :tutorial/content "Recall our solution from the previous slide:\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2\\textrm{i}x} + Be^{-2\\textrm{i}x}.\n$$\n\nWe use **Euler's formula**, which states \n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta,\n$$\n\n to rewrite the exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are typically solving for a real-world quantity, our final answer must be a real-valued function. For this to be true, the arbitrary constants $A$ and $B$ must be a complex conjugate pair ($B = \\overline{A}$). This implies that the following definitions yield *real* constants $P$ and $Q$:\n\n$$\nP = A+B \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q = \\textrm{i}(A-B).\n$$\n\nThis gives the **general real-valued solution**:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = P\\cos{2x} + Q\\sin{2x}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary real constants determined by initial conditions.\n\nSo, for example,\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\cos{2x} - 2\\sin{2x},\n$$\n\ncorresponding to $P=1$ and $Q=-2,$ is a solution of the given differential equation. On the other hand,\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\cos{2x} + \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis *not* a solution because it contains a function $\\sin{x},$ which is not included in the above form representing the general solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "b126cbc0-4b1c-5a98-9c99-005a476f53c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14888 :tutorial/title "Convert the Complex Solution to a Real-Valued Solution"} :step/id #uuid "21a255e2-1f22-52e2-a2f7-4dcfefdf1043" :step/math-academy-id 607953 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088666}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087945}]}
